    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pepin-of-herstal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pepin-of-herstal.jpg" alt="Pepin of Herstal" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pepin-of-herstal">Pepin of Herstal</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Hubert_of_Li%C3%A8ge_offers_his_services_to_Pepin_of_Heristal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">David Aubert (scribe), Loyset Li‚det (illuminator), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Carolingian Empire was built on Frankish power accumulated over three generations of the Carolingian family before its most famous member came to the throne. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-of-herstal">Pepin of Herstal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a> — whose victory near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> in 732 CE checked an Umayyad raid into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> — and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a> transformed the Carolingians from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayor-of-the-palace">palace mayors</a> into the effective rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish kingdom</a>, before Pepin deposed the last Merovingian king, Childeric III, and was made king with papal approval in 751 CE. This papal-Carolingian alliance — in which the Carolingians protected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> while the papacy lent religious legitimacy to Carolingian rule — became a foundational partnership of medieval European history and set the stage for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s coronation. Charlemagne (r. 768–814) inherited only part of the Frankish kingdom on Pepin's death in 768, sharing it with his brother Carloman under the Frankish custom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partible-inheritance">partible inheritance</a>. Carloman's early death in 771 ended the division, and Charlemagne promptly took over his brother's lands. There followed one of the most sustained programmes of conquest in early medieval history: the Lombard kingdom was absorbed in 774, when Charlemagne crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-adrian-i">Pope Adrian I</a>'s request, deposed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desiderius">Desiderius</a> and took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-the-lombards">King of the Lombards</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxon-wars">Saxon Wars</a> (772–804) subjected the pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> through more than thirty years of brutal campaigning, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">mass deportations</a> and forced Christianisation; and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avar-campaign">Avar campaigns</a> of the 790s destroyed the power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a> khaganate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> and brought back treasure so vast — reportedly fifteen wagons, each drawn by four oxen — that it transformed Carolingian finances. On Christmas Day 800 CE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-iii">Pope Leo III</a> crowned Charlemagne in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-peters-basilica">St. Peter's Basilica</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where he was acclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperator-romanorum">Imperator Romanorum</a>, making him the first emperor in the West since the deposition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus-augustulus">Romulus Augustulus</a> in 476 CE and establishing the ideal of a unified Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> under Carolingian leadership.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rhine"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rhine.jpg" alt="Rhine" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Middle_Bridge,_Basel,_Switzerland.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lucazzitto, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Carolingian economy was predominantly agrarian, organised around the great estates (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/villa-estate">villae</a>) of the crown, the church and the aristocracy, which were the principal units of both agricultural production and local lordship. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulary">capitulary</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulare-de-villis">De Villis</a>, generally attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s reign, gives an extraordinarily detailed picture of how a royal estate was expected to function: it specified the crops to be grown, the livestock to be kept, the crafts to be practised and the accounts to be rendered to the king — revealing both the empire's administrative ambitions and its reliance on local self-sufficiency. Trade existed but was constrained by poor infrastructure: rivers such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> were the main commercial arteries, linking inland markets, episcopal cities and monastic fairs to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> trading centres such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorestad">Dorestad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quentovic">Quentovic</a>, where Frisian merchants dominated. Charlemagne's monetary reforms, building on Pepin's, standardised a heavier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> penny (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denarius">denarius</a>), struck at 240 to the pound and issued under royal control across the empire — a significant achievement that facilitated exchange across a territory that had previously used a patchwork of coinages, and the basis of the pound-shilling-penny system used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> until 1971. Agriculture was slowly expanding onto new land through forest clearance, a process that would accelerate after the empire's end and feed the great medieval agricultural expansion of the tenth to twelfth centuries. The empire's fiscal base was relatively thin: royal revenues derived mainly from the king's own estates, war booty (the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a> treasure being an extraordinary windfall), tolls on river and road traffic, and annual gifts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and abbots — enough to sustain the court and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/missi-dominici">missi dominici</a>, but insufficient to maintain an army independent of aristocratic military service.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/saxon-wars"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saxon-wars.jpg" alt="Saxon Wars" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/saxon-wars">Saxon Wars</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frankish_Empire_481_to_814-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sémhur, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s military achievement — the conquest of much of Western and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a> in a reign of nearly half a century — rested on well-equipped mounted warriors, a disciplined logistics system and the political skill to combine military pressure with dynastic marriage and church patronage in a comprehensive strategy of territorial consolidation. The Carolingian army was organised around the obligation of free landowners to provide armed service — the wealthiest serving mounted, lesser men combining their resources to equip one warrior between them — supplemented by the retinues of the great nobles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and abbots, who owed service in proportion to their lands. The annual assembly was a central rhythm of Carolingian government: court, nobility and army gathered each spring or early summer at the general assembly, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campus-martius">Marchfield</a> (Campus Martius) and later the Mayfield, received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulary">capitularies</a> and orders for the year, and set out on campaign. Carolingian logistics were notably sophisticated: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulare-de-villis">De Villis</a> required royal estates to keep carts and supplies ready for the host, reflecting an understanding that armies could be destroyed by supply failure as readily as by the enemy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxon-wars">Saxon Wars</a> (772–804) showed both the army's strengths and its limits: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> could be beaten in battle but melted into forests and marshes, requiring thirty-two years of near-annual campaigns, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">mass deportations</a>, and episodes of exemplary brutality such as the massacre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verden">Verden</a> in 782 before resistance was finally broken. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-roncevaux-pass">Battle of Roncevaux Pass</a> (778), in which Charlemagne's rearguard under the margrave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roland-lord-of-galloway">Roland</a> was ambushed and destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a> during the withdrawal from an unsuccessful Spanish campaign, showed that the Carolingian war machine had geographical limits. The empire had no professional standing army: its military capacity was the sum of obligations owed by free landowners and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a>, and as power passed to a hereditary aristocracy after the empire's partition, the military system became an instrument of noble power rather than royal control.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charlemagne.jpg" alt="Charlemagne" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charlemagne_denier_Mayence_812_814.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Charlemagne. Autorité émettrice de monnaie indéterminé. Atelier monétaire. Émetteur, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Carolingian government was the most ambitious administrative project in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> since the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a>, combining written legislation, a royal inspection system, the administrative capacity of the church and a programme of educational reform into a coherent imperial administration. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulary">capitularies</a> — written royal decrees, often issued at assemblies of the Frankish nobility and clergy — provided the legal framework for governing an enormous territory, addressing everything from the management of royal estates to the conduct of clergy and the standards expected of local judges. Day-to-day authority in the localities lay with some several hundred counts, royal appointees drawn from the aristocracy who held courts, raised troops and collected dues. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/missi-dominici">missi dominici</a> — royal envoys sent out in pairs, usually one layman and one churchman, to tour assigned districts, hear complaints, check the conduct of counts and report back to the palace, a system formalised in 802 — were the main link between central authority and local administration, and were notably effective under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-renaissance">Carolingian Renaissance</a> — the programme of educational and cultural renewal associated with the palace school at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> and with scholars such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcuin-of-york">Alcuin of York</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodulf-of-orleans">Theodulf of Orléans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-the-deacon">Paul the Deacon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/einhard">Einhard</a> — spread the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-minuscule">Carolingian minuscule</a> script that preserved classical texts and eased administrative correspondence, reformed the liturgy and church music, and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> learning the medium of government and religious life. The empire's structural weakness was the Frankish custom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partible-inheritance">partible inheritance</a>, which made long-term unity almost impossible: a custom suited to a warrior aristocracy, in which a father's realm was divided among his legitimate sons, could not coexist with an empire whose coherence depended on a single central authority. Every capable Carolingian ruler spent considerable energy managing the competing claims of sons, brothers and nephews, and no institutional mechanism was ever found to resolve this contradiction.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/oaths-of-strasbourg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oaths-of-strasbourg.jpg" alt="Oaths of Strasbourg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/oaths-of-strasbourg">Oaths of Strasbourg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Serments_de_Strasbourg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Renardeau.arctique, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Carolingian Empire's impact on European history lay primarily in the institutional, cultural and ideological frameworks it established during its brief unified phase rather than in any lasting territorial legacy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-renaissance">Carolingian Renaissance</a> copied and transmitted a large share of the classical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> texts that survive today, standardised Latin as the language of administration and liturgy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Western Christendom</a>, and created the scribal tradition that kept Roman learning alive through the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> until its fuller recovery in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance-of-the-12th-century">twelfth-century Renaissance</a>. The close partnership between counts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, and the empire's reliance on cathedral and monastic centres for administration and education, shaped the ecclesiastical geography of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> and strengthened the network of cathedral cities that would serve as hubs of commerce and culture for centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s coronation in 800 CE established the precedent of a Western imperial title that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> would claim to continue from 962 until 1806, and the ideal of a single Christian empire governing Latin Christendom remained a powerful theme of European political thought throughout the Middle Ages and beyond. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> (843 CE) divided the empire into kingdoms that foreshadowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaths-of-strasbourg">Oaths of Strasbourg</a> (842 CE), sworn in both a Romance vernacular and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-high-german">Old High German</a>, are the earliest surviving text in a language recognisable as early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-french">Old French</a>. Few divisions in European political history proved more consequential for the diplomacy and warfare of later centuries. Charlemagne's exchange of embassies with the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> — which brought the elephant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-abbas">Abul-Abbas</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> in 802 — established an early precedent of diplomatic contact between Latin Christendom and the Islamic world.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fontenoy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-fontenoy.jpg" alt="Battle of Fontenoy" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fontenoy">Battle of Fontenoy</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pierre_Lenfant_-_La_Bataille_de_Fontenoy,_le_11_mai_1745_(Ch%C3%A2teau_de_Versailles).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pierre L'Enfant, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Carolingian Empire's decline was built into its foundations by the custom that would eventually destroy it: the Frankish practice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partible-inheritance">partible inheritance</a>, which divided a father's realm among his surviving legitimate sons. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> was fortunate that two of his three legitimate adult sons, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-younger">Charles the Younger</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-of-italy">Pepin of Italy</a>, died before him, allowing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> to inherit the undivided empire in 814 CE — but such fortune could not be relied upon. Louis held the empire together through a reign of twenty-six years in which he was twice deposed by his own sons, most humiliatingly at the “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-of-lies">Field of Lies</a>” near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colmar">Colmar</a> in 833 CE, when his army deserted to the rebels en masse, and was constantly renegotiating the division of the empire among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a>, Pepin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> and, after 823, his youngest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>. The civil war that broke out after Louis's death in 840 CE was the logical outcome of these disputes: at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fontenoy">Battle of Fontenoy</a> (841 CE), Carolingian armies fought one another in a battle remembered by contemporaries for its terrible slaughter. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> (843 CE) formalised a threefold division into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a> under Charles the Bald, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Francia</a> (from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>) under Lothair, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> under Louis the German, ending the unified empire in practice even as the imperial title passed with the Middle Kingdom. The following decades saw further subdivision as sons and grandsons partitioned their inheritances, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids devastated coastal and riverine regions and exposed the inability of divided kingdoms to coordinate their defence, and the nobility accumulated hereditary local power at the expense of the crown. The brief reunion of all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish kingdoms</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-fat">Charles the Fat</a> (884–887) proved hollow: during the Viking siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> (885–886) he bought the attackers off rather than fight, an act widely seen as cowardice, and he was deposed in November 887 and died in January 888, after which the empire fragmented permanently into the kingdoms from which the states of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> would grow.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Carolingian Empire's most enduring legacy was not the territory it briefly unified but the idea it implanted: that a single Christian political authority could encompass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Europe</a> — an idea that inspired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>'s universal claims and later visions of European unity, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> the emblematic father of a continent whose unity he briefly achieved and whose aspiration to unity his example permanently shaped.</em></p>

